<h2>How to Use Your Generator Safely During Hurricane Season</h2>
<img alt="" class="attachment-full size-full wp-image-7182" height="1793" loading="lazy" src="/assets/original/2024/08/generator_hurricane_safety.jpg" width="2391">
<p>Living in Florida, it’s a common occurrence to experience power outages due to hurricanes and severe weather conditions. To combat this, many homeowners invest in portable generators to keep their homes running during an outage. However, with great convenience comes great responsibility! Safety should always be a top priority when it comes to operating a generator. In this post, we will discuss the importance of following safety protocols, avoiding common mistakes, understanding the impact of a power outage, and choosing a generator with multiple safety features.</p>
<h2>Keep the Generator Outside</h2>
<p>Following safety protocols is crucial when operating a generator. Before starting the generator, it is important to set it up in a well-ventilated area away from doors or windows. Make sure to read the manufacturer’s instructions carefully before handling fuel and oils. Keep fuel stored in a safe and well-ventilated area and fill the tanks outside or in a well-ventilated area away from any sources of ignition.</p>
<figure><img alt="" loading="lazy" src="https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcT1QU9GAqYPBUMQYbdAJOR1M8pCzoANmw7xBA&amp;usqp=CAU"/></figure>
<h2>Carbon Monoxide</h2>
<p>Another crucial aspect of generator safety is preventing electrical hazards and carbon monoxide poisoning. Ensure that appliances and extension cords are properly grounded. </p>
<p>Carbon monoxide is an odorless and tasteless gas that can be fatal if inhaled in high concentrations. Always ensure that the generator is placed outside and away from open windows and doors. </p>
<p>Installing a battery-powered carbon monoxide detector in or near the sleeping area can also help alert you to any potential danger.</p>
<figure><img alt="generator safety" class="wp-image-5236" loading="lazy" src="/assets/original/2023/05/generator_hurricane_safety-1024x768.jpg"/></figure>
<h2>Beware Overloading</h2>
<p>Common mistakes can be detrimental when it comes to operating a generator. Overloading the generator or running it indoors can cause a plethora of problems. Overloading can lead to a malfunctioning generator or even a fire. Running a generator indoors can cause carbon monoxide poisoning and electrocution. Improper storage and neglecting maintenance can also lead to safety issues. Properly store your generator in a dry and secure area and conduct regular maintenance checks to ensure it’s in good working condition.</p>
<p>Understanding the impact of a power outage on daily life is vital when it comes to prioritizing devices to use during an outage. Medical devices, refrigeration, and heating/cooling systems should be given priority to keep your household running smoothly. Understanding the limitations of your generator is also important. </p>
<p>Always <a href="http://localhost:8082/wise-counsel/how-to-calculate-how-much-power-your-generator-will-need/">choose a generator with a wattage capacity that will meet your needs</a>. It’s also important to have a backup plan in case of longer outages, such as a secondary generator or an alternate power source.</p>
<p>Choosing a generator with multiple safety features is essential. Look for generators with automatic shutdown features when low oil levels are detected or when it’s overloaded. </p>
<p>Other helpful features include an electric start function and built-in circuit breakers. Creating a maintenance and use checklist can also help you keep track of generator maintenance and usage. Store your generator safely in a dry and secure area, protected from the elements and theft.</p>
<p>Operating a generator can make all the difference during a power outage, but it’s important to prioritize safety at all times. Following safety protocols, avoiding common mistakes, understanding the impact of a power outage, and choosing a generator with multiple safety features are all crucial aspects of generator safety. </p>
<p>Remember to conduct regular maintenance checks and always have a backup plan in case of longer outages. With these safety measures in place, you can rest assured that you and your family will stay safe during a power outage.</p>
